{ The Colle System. White builds a solid set-up with d4, Nf3, e3, Bd3 and c3, castles, and then plays e4 at the right moment. The pieces come to the same squares whatever Black does, so it is easy to learn, but it only works if you actually play the e4 break; without it the position goes dead. You play White. }
1. d4
{ [%eval 0.25] White takes space in the centre and opens the c1 bishop and the queen. }
1... d5 { [%eval 0.27] Black does the same. } 2. Nf3
{ [%eval 0.26] Develops a knight, defends d4 and prepares to castle. } 2... Nf6
{ [%eval 0.27] Black develops. } ( 2... Bf5
{ [%eval 0.34] Black brings the bishop out before ...e6, the anti-Colle idea. White must be ready to trade bishops with Bd3 and keep the pawn structure healthy. }
3. e3 { [%eval 0.07] White still plays the Colle. } 3... e6
{ [%eval 0.08] Black prepares to develop. } 4. Bd3
{ [%eval 0.02] White challenges the bishop, which can be traded. } 4... Bxd3
{ [%eval 0.17] Black trades. } 5. Qxd3
{ [%eval 0.19] White recaptures with the queen, leaving the pawn structure healthy. }
